    "body": "A missing `contentnamespace` means \"inherit from where I'm parsed\" rather than html. So if the serializer omits it, serializing and reparsing can change the namespace of the template contents, which is an mXSS vector.\n\nI looked at omitting the attribute only when the state is html and the parent is in the HTML namespace. It needs exceptions: `<template for>` takes its namespace from the patch target, nested templates have a DocumentFragment parent, the parent may not be part of the serialization, and the parent may not round-trip itself. Storing the namespace on the `template` element and always serializing it seems simpler. Things to get right with that:\n\n- The serializer emits the stored state, not the attribute's value, and skips any other attribute that serializes as `contentnamespace`. That covers an invalid value, a value changed after parsing, and `setAttributeNS()` with a non-null namespace and no prefix. If it emits the state first, the parser's duplicate-attribute handling keeps it. (Or maybe the serializer can drop namespaced `contentnamespace` attributes?)\n- Templates not created by the HTML parser (e.g. `createElement()`, the XML parser) get html, and the state is copied by cloning steps (#13100 currently has neither).\n- Fragment parsing with a `template` context element (`setHTML()` and friends) uses the stored state.\n- Does setting or removing the `contentnamespace` attribute after creation update the stored state? If not, `getAttribute()` and `contentNamespace` can disagree.\n- `shadowrootmode` overrides `contentnamespace`, so a `<template shadowrootmode>` whose shadow root wasn't attached (e.g. it came from `innerHTML`) and has html state, moved into `<svg>`, still reparses with SVG contents, because attaching to an svg host fails. Maybe `contentnamespace` should apply when the declarative shadow root isn't attached? Supporting svg hosts wouldn't be enough, since attaching can fail for other reasons (e.g. `innerHTML`, or the host already has a shadow root).\n\nHowever, this means `innerHTML` output changes for every existing template (an added `contentnamespace=\"html\"`), which might be a web compat risk.\n",
